During the period of the [[Mauryan]] and [[Gupta Empire|Gupta]] empires and their successors, several Buddhist architectural complexes, such as the caves of [[Ajanta Caves|Ajanta]] and [[Ellora Caves|Ellora]] and the monumental [[Sanchi]] [[Stupa]] were built. Later on, South India produced several Hindu temples like [[Chennakeshava Temple, Belur|Chennakesava Temple]] at [[Belur, Karnataka|Belur]], the [[Hoysaleswara temple|Hoysaleswara Temple]] at [[Halebidu]], and the [[Chennakesava Temple at Somanathapura|Kesava Temple]] at [[Somanathapura, Mysuru|Somanathapura]], [[Brihadeeswara Temple]], [[Thanjavur]] built by Raja Raja Chola, the [[Sun Temple]], [[Konark]], [[Sri Ranganathaswamy Temple (Srirangam)|Sri Ranganathaswamy Temple]] at [[Srirangam]], and the [[Gautama Buddha|Buddha]] [[stupa]] (Chinna Lanja dibba and Vikramarka kota dibba) at [[Bhattiprolu]]. [[Rajput kingdoms]] oversaw the construction of [[Khajuraho Group of Monuments|Khajuraho Temple Complex]], [[Chittor Fort]] and [[Chaturbhuj Temple (Orchha)|Chaturbhuj Temple]], etc. during their reign. [[Angkor Wat]], Borobudur and other [[Buddhist]] and [[Hindu]] temples indicate strong Indian influence on South East Asian architecture, as they are built in styles almost identical to traditional Indian religious buildings.

The traditional system of [[Vaastu Shastra]] serves as India's version of [[Feng Shui]], influencing town planning, architecture, and ergonomics. It is unclear which system is older, but they contain certain similarities. Feng Shui is more commonly used throughout the world. Though Vastu is conceptually similar to Feng Shui in that it also tries to harmonise the flow of energy, (also called life-force or [[Prana]] in Sanskrit and [[Qi|Chi]]/[[Qi|Ki]] in Chinese/Japanese), through the house, it differs in the details, such as the exact directions in which various objects, rooms, materials, etc. are to be placed.

With the advent of Islamic influence from the west, Indian architecture was adapted to allow the traditions of the new religion, creating the [[Indo-Islamic architecture|Indo-Islamic]] style of architecture. The [[Qutb Minar complex|Qutb complex]], a group of monuments constructed by successive sultanas of the [[Delhi Sultanate]] is one of the earliest examples. [[Fatehpur Sikri]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/255|title=Fatehpur Sikri|last=Centre|first=UNESCO World Heritage|website=whc.unesco.org|language=en|access-date=17 November 2018|archive-date=14 May 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514052508/https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/255|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Taj Mahal]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252|title=Taj Mahal|last=Centre|first=UNESCO World Heritage|website=whc.unesco.org|language=en|access-date=17 November 2018|archive-date=15 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210315231759/http://whc.unesco.org/en/list/252|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Gol Gumbaz]], [[Red Fort of Delhi]]<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/231|title=Red Fort Complex|last=Centre|first=UNESCO World Heritage|website=whc.unesco.org|language=en|access-date=17 November 2018|archive-date=3 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090803141206/http://whc.unesco.org/en/list/231|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Charminar]] are creations of this era, and are often used as the stereotypical symbols of India.

British colonial rule in India saw the development of [[Indo-Saracenic]] style and mixing of several other styles, such as European Gothic. The [[Victoria Memorial (India)|Victoria Memorial]] and the [[Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus]] are notable examples.

Indian architecture has influenced eastern and southeastern Asia, due to the spread of Buddhism. A number of Indian architectural features such as the temple mound or [[stupa]], temple spire or [[shikhara]], temple tower or [[pagoda]] and temple gate or [[torana]], have become famous symbols of Asian culture, used extensively in [[East Asia]] and [[South East Asia]]. The central spire is also sometimes called a [[Gopuram|vimanam]]. The southern temple gate, or [[gopuram]] is noted for its intricacy and majesty.

Contemporary [[Architecture of India|Indian architecture]] is more cosmopolitan. Cities are extremely compact and densely populated. Mumbai's [[Nariman Point]] is famous for its [[Art Deco]] buildings. Recent creations such as the [[Lotus Temple]],<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5921/|title=Bahá'í House of Worship at New Delhi - UNESCO World Heritage Centre|last=Centre|first=UNESCO World Heritage|website=whc.unesco.org|language=en|access-date=17 November 2018|archive-date=26 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160826220742/https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5921/|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Golden Pagoda, Namsai|Golden Pagoda]] and [[Akshardham (Delhi)|Akshardham]], and the various modern urban developments of India like [[Bhubaneswar]] and [[Chandigarh]], are notable.

