[[Field hockey]] was considered to be the national game of India, but this has been recently denied by the [[Government of India]], clarifying on a [[Right to Information Act|Right to Information Act (RTI)]] filed that India has not declared any sport as the national game.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hindustantimes.com/India-news/NewDelhi/No-records-to-state-hockey-as-national-game/Article1-907069.aspx |title=No records to state hockey as national game |newspaper=Hindustan Times |date=2 August 2012 |access-date=1 March 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130105023836/http://www.hindustantimes.com/India-news/NewDelhi/No-records-to-state-hockey-as-national-game/Article1-907069.aspx |archive-date=5 January 2013  }}</ref><ref name="Gooptu">{{cite journal|title=Cricket or cricket spectacle? Looking beyond cricket to understand Lagaan |first=S |last=Gooptu |journal=The International Journal of the History of Sport|year=2004}}</ref><ref name="Karafin">{{cite book |title=South India |first1=Amy |last1=Karafin |first2=Anirban |last2=Mahapatra |year=2009 |publisher=Lonely Planet|page=69}}</ref> At a time when it was especially popular, the [[India men's national field hockey team]] won the 1975 [[Hockey World Cup|Men's Hockey World Cup]], and 8 [[Gold medal|gold]], 1 [[Silver medal|silver]], and 2 [[Bronze medal|bronze]] medals at the Olympic Games. However, field hockey in India no longer has the following that it once did.<ref name="Karafin"/>

[[Cricket]] is by far the most popular sport in India.<ref name="Gooptu"/> The [[India national cricket team]] won the [[1983 Cricket World Cup]], the [[2011 Cricket World Cup]], the [[2007 ICC World Twenty20|2007 T20 World Cup]], the [[2024 Men's T20 World Cup|2024 T20 World Cup]], the [[2002 ICC Champions Trophy]], the [[2013 ICC Champions Trophy]] and the [[2025 ICC Champions Trophy]]. Domestic competitions include the [[Ranji Trophy]], the [[Duleep Trophy]], the [[Deodhar Trophy]], the [[Irani Trophy]] and the [[NKP Salve Challenger Trophy|Challenger Series]]. In addition, [[Board of Control for Cricket in India|BCCI]] conducts the [[Indian Premier League]], a [[Twenty20]] competition.

[[association football|Football]] is popular in the Indian states of Kerala, Goa, West Bengal and the Northeastern states. The city of [[Kolkata]] is the home to the largest stadium in [[India]], and the second [[List of stadiums by capacity|largest stadium in the world]] by capacity, [[Salt Lake Stadium]], as well as clubs such as [[Mohun Bagan A.C.]], [[Kingfisher East Bengal F.C.]], [[Prayag United S.C.]], and the [[Mohammedan Sporting Club (Kolkata)|Mohammedan Sporting Club]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Mohun Bagan vs East Bengal: India's all-consuming rivalry |url=https://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/stories/classicderby/news/newsid=1414458.html |publisher=[[FIFA]] |access-date=7 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111122032920/http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/stories/classicderby/news/newsid%3D1414458.html |archive-date=22 November 2011  }}</ref>

[[Chess]] is commonly believed to have originated in northwestern India during the [[Gupta Empire|Gupta empire]],<ref name=Leibs92>Leibs (2004), p. 92</ref><ref name=RobinsonEstes34>Robinson & Estes (1996), p. 34</ref><ref name="Murray">{{cite book
 | last=Murray
 | first=H. J. R.
 | author-link=H. J. R. Murray
 | title=A History of Chess
 | publisher=Benjamin Press (originally published by Oxford University Press)
 | year=1913
 | isbn=978-0-936317-01-4
 | oclc=13472872
 | url=https://archive.org/details/historyofchess00murr
 }}</ref><ref>Bird 1893, p. 63</ref> where its early form in the 6th century was known as ''[[chaturanga]]''. Other games which [[Traditional games of India|originated in India]] and continue to remain popular in wide parts of northern India include [[Kabaddi]], [[Gilli-Danda]], and [[Kho kho]]. Traditional southern Indian games include [[Snake boat race]] and [[Kuttiyum kolum]]. The modern game of [[polo]] is derived from [[Manipur, India]], where the game was known as 'Sagol Kangjei', 'Kanjai-bazee', or 'Pulu'.<ref name="manipurpolo">{{cite web |title=Polo History |url=http://www.indiapolo.com/Polopedia/History/history.html |access-date=2 January 2019 |archive-date=28 March 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090328122313/http://www.indiapolo.com/Polopedia/History/history.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.indianpolo.com/manipurpolo/history.asp |title=Manipur Polo – Indianpolo.com, polo, polo in india |publisher=Indianpolo.com |date=25 March 2007 |access-date=25 January 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190211023839/http://www.indianpolo.com/manipurpolo/history.asp |archive-date=11 February 2019 }}</ref> It was the anglicised form of the last, referring to the wooden ball that was used, which was adopted by the sport in its slow spread to the west. The first polo club was established in the town of [[Silchar]] in Assam, India, in 1833.

In 2011, India inaugurated a privately built [[Buddh International Circuit]], its first motor racing circuit. The 5.14-kilometre circuit is in [[Greater Noida]], [[Uttar Pradesh]], near [[Delhi]]. The first [[Formula One]] [[Indian Grand Prix]] event was hosted here in October 2011.<ref>[http://zeenews.india.com/sports/motorsports/buddh-international-circuit-unveiled-amidst-cheers_731009.html "Buddh International Circuit unveiled amidst cheers"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160430063836/http://zeenews.india.com/sports/motorsports/buddh-international-circuit-unveiled-amidst-cheers_731009.html |date=30 April 2016 }} 19 October 2011, Zee News</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2011/10/12704.html|work=Formula1.com|publisher=[[Formula One Group|Formula One Administration]]|date=28 October 2011|access-date=28 October 2011|title=India: Friday practice – selected team and driver quotes|archive-date=4 July 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140704005156/http://www.formula1.com/news/headlines/2011/10/12704.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

